  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 635 AM MST Sun Nov 15 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA mild and dry southwesterly flow aloft has reestablished across \nthe Northern Rockies and MT and will remain in place through \nMonday. Starting Tuesday, a series of Pacific storm systems will \npush large-scale troughing inland, bringing a transition toward \ncooler and more active weather that persists into next weekend. \n\nWell-above-average temperatures will persist through Monday. \nBreezy to locally windy conditions are anticipated across much of \nthe region this afternoon, driven by a moderate surface pressure \ngradient combined with 30-40 kt 700 MB southwesterly flow. Peak \nafternoon atmospheric mixing will produce wind gusts near 30 mph \nin most areas. However, local terrain effects will enhance winds \nalong the immediate eastern slopes of the Rocky Mountain Front and\nwithin terrain-aligned valleys of Southwest Montana, generating \nhigher sustained winds of 30-40 mph and localized gusts reaching \nup to 50 mph. \n\nAs the initial upper-level trough moves inland early this week, \nit will slow and split. This split allows a surface low to track \nnortheastward out of Wyoming late Tuesday, while the northern \nsegment of the trough lingers over Western Montana. Consequently, \na region of mid-level deformation and frontogenesis will develop \nand progress eastward across the region late Tuesday and Tuesday \nnight. An accompanying plume of anomalous Pacific moisture will \nsupport locally moderate precipitation as the upper trough and \nfrontogenic forcing cross the area late Tuesday and Tuesday night.\nThe WY surface low will pull cooler low-level air southward along\nthe Rocky Mountain Front on Tuesday, followed by broader large- \nscale cooling aloft as the main trough crosses the region Tuesday \nnight, driving snow levels down below 4,000 feet across much of \nthe area before precipitation winds down by Wednesday morning.\n\nLarge scale troughing lingers across the western US later this\nweek into next weekend with another distinct upper low/trough\nmoving inland and potentially affecting the area by Friday.\nOverall cooler/seasonal conditions will be maintained with\nprecipitation chances increasing towards the end of the work week.\nModel ensembles lean toward higher precipitation chances across\nsouthern portions of the forecast area with this system but there\nis still a fair amount of uncertainty with details. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nPrecipitation Potential (Tuesday & Tuesday Night): Recent model\nensemble guidance maintains reasonable support for higher \nprecipitation amounts focused along the I-15 corridor, where \nprobabilities for exceeding 0.50\" reach 40-50% (as well as across \nmuch of Southwest MT). High-resolution models indicate that initial \nfrontogenic banding will develop further west near the Continental \nDivide during the day Tuesday. A secondary zone of frontogenesis is \nprojected to consolidate over the I-15 corridor Tuesday evening \nbefore shifting eastward and diminishing overnight. Because this \nevent remains over 48 hours out, additional minor adjustments to \nthese spatial and timing details are likely.\n\nSnow Levels & Accumulation: Snow levels will start out around 4,000- \n6,000 ft across the region as precipitation begins Tuesday \nafternoon. They are expected to fall rapidly to near or below 4,000 \nft along and west of the I-15 corridor in north-central MT as a \nnarrow shallow surge of cooler air slides south along the Rocky \nMountain Front. By Tuesday evening and overnight, snow levels will \ndrop below 4,000 ft across most of the area. There is a 60-70% \nchance of snowfall totals reaching 2 inches or more across most \nmountain passes, with up to a 30% probability of amounts reaching 4 \ninches over Rogers and McDonald Passes where mesoscale banding is \nmore likely to develop. Confidence in low- elevation snow \naccumulations remains lower given marginal surface temperatures and \nlingering uncertainty regarding the exact timing of the lower snow \nlevels.",
